# Intent: tell which side a sound comes from (owner mission 2026-11-12, "can the StackChan hear direction"). The CoreS3 has two front microphones on the ES7210 (MIC1 and MIC2; MIC3 is the speaker loopback, not read) and the firmware already reads both as the two input channels, so no codec change is needed. The audio input task hands every chunk it reads (24 kHz, two channels interleaved, before the 16 kHz resampler) to maipai_sound_tap, which only adds up sums: each channel's energy and their cross-correlation for lags of -8..8 samples (333 us, 11.4 cm of path). Every 100 ms a small worker task turns the sums into features: the RMS of each channel, the lag of the correlation peak (sub-sample by parabolic interpolation; positive when the sound reached channel 0 first), a confidence (the normalised peak, 0..1) and the level difference in dB. Output: a serial trace "SD <ms> <rms0> <rms1> <lag_x100> <conf_x100> <db_x10>" for every window above the quiet floor (RMS 40) in the first half hour after boot, a "sound_dir" event (l, r, lag, conf, db, rate) at most every 1.5 s while a confident (0.5) sound louder than RMS 120 is heard, and a "sound_stats" diagnostic once a minute (windows, loud, confident, lag mean/min/max, conf max, dropped). The host maps lag and level to a side; the firmware does not know where the microphones are. Cost: about 0.4 M multiply-adds a second in the audio task, static buffers only (its stack is 6 KB), one 4 KB task in PSRAM.
# Upstream: m5stack/StackChan firmware/main/hal/hal.cpp, hal_mcp.cpp, hal/board/cores3_audio_codec.cc (MIT); new file hal_sound_dir.cpp. Applies after 081 with --unidiff-zero (the hal_mcp.cpp hunk sits after 081's front-tap push). Pairs with 082-sound-tap.patch on xiaozhi-esp32.
# Check after a flash: the boot log says "sound direction on: 17 lags, 100 ms windows"; a clap or music beside the unit prints SD lines on the serial console and the host logs "unit sound: sound_dir l=.. r=.. lag=.. conf=.. db=.."; a quiet room prints nothing and the minute's sound_stats has loud near 0; the wake word and chat work as before.
